review:
The good;
I loved it, its so much fun, the puzzles are beautifully designed and the game is perfect. The proximity chat is a little bit too restrictive I think on distance but its not a huge impediment to the enjoyment of the game. Its also a really good length. It doesn't outstay its welcome or leave you wanting more. It's found a really nice balance.

The bad;
no AI disclosure. I know its only to fix a few lines of code but people just want to make informed decisions on games they buy. The disclosure of its use isn't to lambast and marginalise users of AI it's so that consumers have CHOICE. The anger people felt when they found out about it wasn't because it was used but because they didn't get told.

Didn't post a review after completing the game, but seeing the recent news, I think it's a good time to do so;

This is an amazing chill game and I had a memorable experience with my friends. It was extremely fun planning our journeys, taking in the sights, chatting while on the train or ski-lift, and brainstorming solutions to complex puzzles....would highly recommend it to anyone with a passion for exploring and a good group of friends.

Addressing the "elephant" in the room, the whole unravel of House House turning to AI to assist with debugging ~10 lines of code has certainly been blown out of proportion. Imo, this was a minor breach of principle, it was something that would have been great to announce beforehand, but it is also understandable why it wasn't announced (not required according to Steam, also extremely small contribution when everything else was human-made). We can be anti-AI but acknowledge what is acceptable use of AI and what isn't, and AI helping to debug a stubborn issue that wasn't able to be resolved by Unity is what I consider to be acceptable use; A tool to assist developers, not a replacement for human jobs.

- Don't look at anything else from this game;
- Get a bunch of your friends together;
- Play it as the devs intended (NO CHEATING);
- ??????
- Have the best experience COOP gaming has to offer.

I don't think a game had made me this emotional since Outer Wilds. You can see the devs who worked on this put their heart and soul into making one of the best designed COOP puzzle game I've personally ever played. Do yourself a favor and try it out with the pals.

Great sound design, amazing lighting, phenomenal puzzle designs, breath taking views, awesome atmosphere, fun mechanics, accurate depiction of Aussie bushland. However it's a 0/10 because I couldn't upload the entire 'Bee Movie' script onto the whiteboards.

Anyways here's a link to the entire 'Bee Movie' script;
